Hi,

I'm trying to plot nest density and have been following the example here closely: https://inlabru-org.github.io/inlabru/articles/web/2d_lgcp_sf.html

Would it be possible to provide some example code to illustrate how to extract the value for maximum density of nests on the linear predictor scale?
Thanks!

From uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com  Sat Jul  8 14:01:21 2023
From: uafb003e064114795 at groups.google.com (Finn Lindgren)
Date: Sat, 08 Jul 2023 14:01:21 +0000
Subject: Re: [r-inla] Extracting values from LGCP
Message-ID: <BzOVHUOrAOQ.3_YQIce0AQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <BzOVHUOrAOQ.0Mk9BU6zAQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
References: <BzOVHUOrAOQ.0Mk9BU6zAQAJ@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/BzOVHUOrAOQ/m/3_YQIce0AQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

If you just want a point estimate of the maximum density, then max(pred$mean) would give you that, from a predict call with predictor formula
  ~ exp(the expression for the linear predictor)
I think the vignette already includes such a predict call.

If you want the posterior distribution of the maximum, then using generate() with expression
  ~ max(exp(…))
will give you samples from it.  Using the same formula with predict() will give you summary statistics (posterior mean, std.dev <http://std.dev>, and some quantiles).

Finn

